# Next.js App Router

Reference: [Next.js Docs](https://nextjs.org/docs), [Vercel: Common App Router Mistakes](https://vercel.com/blog/common-mistakes-with-the-next-js-app-router-and-how-to-fix-them)

---

## Server vs Client Components

| Use Server Components for | Use Client Components for |
|---------------------------|---------------------------|
| Data fetching (DB, APIs) | `useState`, `useEffect`, hooks |
| API keys, secrets (never exposed) | Event handlers (`onClick`, etc.) |
| Reducing client JS bundle | Browser APIs (localStorage, geolocation) |
| Progressive streaming | React Context consumers |

**Rule:** Prefer Server Components by default. Add `"use client"` only when needed. Keep the client boundary as low as possible—isolate interactive parts into leaf components.

**Composition:** Children of a Client Component can be Server Components. Pass them as `children` or slots.

```tsx
// ✅ Page stays Server Component; interactive part is Client
export default function Page() {
  return (
    <section>
      <h1>Title</h1>
      <ClientCounter />
    </section>
  );
}
```

---

## File Conventions

| File | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `loading.tsx` | Instant loading UI (Suspense fallback). Server Component by default. Wraps `page.tsx` and children. Use `Loading` from `@/components/ui/loading`. |
| `error.tsx` | Error boundary. **Must** be Client Component (`'use client'`). Props: `error`, `reset()`. Use `error.digest` for server logs. |
| `layout.tsx` | Shared UI; persists during navigation. |
| `page.tsx` | Route page. |
| `template.tsx` | Re-mounts on navigation (unlike layout). |

**loading.tsx example:**
```tsx
import { Loading } from "@/components/ui/loading";

export default function RouteLoading() {
  return <Loading />;
}
```

**error.tsx example:**
```tsx
"use client";

import { useEffect } from "react";
import { Button } from "@/components/ui/button";

export default function Error({
  error,
  reset,
}: {
  error: Error & { digest?: string };
  reset: () => void;
}) {
  useEffect(() => {
    console.error(error);
  }, [error]);

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Algo deu errado</h2>
      <Button onClick={reset}>Tentar novamente</Button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

